# The package set, as depends(), and the only place it is written
# (docs/structure.md P5-P7).
#
# This replaces moarchy-base.packages and moarchy-extras.packages, which the
# old installer read with grep. Every comment in those files explained why a
# package is present or why an upstream one is absent, so they came across with
# the names rather than being summarised away.
#
# The divergence this prevents has already happened once: docker/ built walker
# and elephant while install/build-src.sh did not and moarchy-base.packages
# documented both as unused in 4.x -- three lists, two of them right. One list,
# with pacman resolving it, cannot do that.
#
# No files. `pacman -Ql moarchy-meta` is empty by design.

. "$startdir/../../scripts/manifest.sh"
_ver=$(manifest_get moarchy version) || return 1

pkgname=moarchy-meta
pkgver=$_ver
# The 2 this line carried into 0.1.1 covered everything since the 0.1.1-1 the
# [moarchy] repo publishes: the list gaining mise-bin, nodejs and npm, losing
# lisgd in dafd34f, and gaining the AUR build toolchain below. Three changes on one bump, and that
# is not a waiver being stretched -- the rule is the one pkgbuilds/moarchy
# states since 11b98fe. pkgrel moves when content has changed since the last
# artifact that was PUBLISHED, and packages/ holding a 0.1.1-2 nobody has ever
# installed is not that. Rebuilding it from a newer HEAD under the same version
# hides nothing from anything, because no device holds it.
#
# This paragraph said "built" rather than "published" until now, and briefly
# bought a 3 that nothing needed.
#
# The distinction is worth the words because this package is the one most
# exposed to getting it wrong in the other direction: its entire content IS the
# depends line, so a change here that does not move the version is invisible to
# pacman, which compares versions and not depends.
#
# It has been here before. It was 2 for 0.1.0 as well, after the list gained
# gnome-contacts, geary, alligator, amberol, secrets and songrec and lost kclock
# and index-fm with no version move at all -- so a phone already holding
# 0.1.0-1 would never have seen any of it. Then pkgver moved to 0.1.1 and this
# went back to 1, because 0.1.1-1 is already newer than 0.1.0-2.
# 1 for 0.2.2, and nothing about that is a reset: 0.2.1 shipped as -1 and the
# line never left 1 during the cycle.
#
# The honest statement of what a phone gains here is: nothing. depends= is
# byte-identical to the published 0.2.1-1 -- no package joined the set and none
# left it -- so this package moves to 0.2.2-1 only because the project version
# moved and pkgver reads it. That costs a 3 KB download and keeps the two
# packages that carry this project's version in step, which is worth more than
# the alternative: a moarchy-meta reading 0.2.1 on a phone whose moarchy reads
# 0.2.2, and half an hour spent working out whether an upgrade had failed.
#
# The direction that is easy to get wrong is the other one, and the paragraphs
# above are the record of it: this package's entire content IS the depends
# line, so a name added or REMOVED under an unchanged version is invisible to
# pacman, which compares versions and not depends.
#
# 2 for exactly that reason: 0.2.2-1 is published and this build adds one name
# (adw-gtk-theme, which is half of how a GTK3 app takes the theme --
# docs/style.md I1). Under an unchanged version no phone would ever install it,
# and the half that is already in bin/omarchy-theme-set-gnome would go on
# checking for a theme that is not there.
#
# 3, and the reason is the sharper reading of "published" that the paragraphs
# above turn on. 0.2.2-2 was an unpublished artifact this morning -- nothing
# held it, so it was rebuilt repeatedly under one version, deliberately. The
# 20260913 image ended that: the card was flashed and the phone holds these
# exact bytes now, so "no device holds it" is simply false and the waiver those
# paragraphs describe no longer applies. This build REMOVES six names --
# amberol, alligator, htop, neovim, songrec, wiremix -- and a removal under an
# unchanged version is the invisible case §above names explicitly: the phone
# would keep all six forever, since pacman compares versions and not depends.
#
# It also ADDS two, and the line stays at 3 for both. moarchy-vitals came in at
# the same 3, and gst-plugins-good moved up out of optdepends because amberol
# was the only thing hard-depending on it (see the entry). Neither buys a 4: the
# rule is "changed since the last PUBLISHED artifact", the published one is
# still 0.2.2-1, and 3 is already different from it. A -3 installed by hand on
# the dev phone half an hour ago is not what ended the waiver for -2 either --
# what ended that was a flashed IMAGE, which is an artifact that leaves the
# building. Reinstalling the same version over itself on a phone I am holding
# costs one `pacman -U` and hides nothing from anyone.
#
# 4: the default calculator, calendar, contacts, file manager and clocks are
# the moarchy-apps Quickshell plugins now, not kalk / calindori /
# gnome-contacts / portfolio-file-manager / gnome-clocks. GTK Keep and Vitals
# leave with them, because the plugins are the same apps (same notes file,
# same /proc reader) and two tiles for one job is the thing this list has
# been deleting since kclock. A removal under an unchanged version is the
# invisible case the paragraphs above exist for: the phone would keep all
# seven forever. The plugin tree in moarchy is what replaces them.
#
# 5: kweather leaves too. The Weather plugin is already in moarchy,
# and two weather tiles is the clocks cut again. The phone holds 0.2.2-4, so
# a removal under that number would never be offered.
#
# 6 was never built under that number; it ships as 0.3.0-1, below.
# gnome-text-editor leaves. The moarchy.editor plugin in
# moarchy is the editor now -- both what opens a tapped text file and
# $EDITOR, through moarchy-editor --wait -- and a phone holding 0.2.2-5 would
# keep GNOME Text Editor as a second tile for the same job, for ever.
# gnome-calls, chatty and mmsd-tng leave in the same unbuilt release, for
# moarchy.phone and moarchy.messages. A phone left holding Calls beside
# Phone has two things answering one modem, which is not a spare tile but a
# call picked up in whichever window was quicker.
# loupe, papers, foliate and secrets leave in it too, so the default set is the
# shell's plugins plus what a phone cannot do without, and chipass stops
# arriving behind geary (the note at gnome-keyring says how it got in).
# geary leaves in the same release, for moarchy.mail. A phone holding
# 0.2.2-5 would keep Geary beside Mail: two tiles, two things holding the same
# password, and the one of them that waits on a keyring prompt it cannot show.
#
# 1 for 0.3.0. pkgver moved, and 0.3.0-1 is newer than the 0.2.2-5 the
# 20260915 images carry, so every removal listed under 6 still reaches a phone
# that holds it -- as a depends line that no longer names the package. pacman
# -Syu does not uninstall what leaves this list; `pacman -Rs` does.
pkgrel=1
pkgdesc="The moarchy package set -- install this and you have the phone"
arch=('any')
url='https://github.com/SimonSchubert/moarchy'
license=('MIT')

depends=(
  # The aarch64 package set. Most of it is Arch Linux ARM; megapixels comes
  # from [danctnix]
  # (archmobile.mirror.danctnix.org), which the DanctNIX image's pacman.conf
  # carries and a bare ALARM install does not. The list this replaced claimed
  # at the top that every entry was on archlinuxarm.org, which was not true of
  # megapixels or mmsd-tng -- `pacman -U --print` in a bare container is what
  # found it. It was three until lisgd left with bin/moarchy-gestures, two
  # until portfolio-file-manager left with the Files plugin, and one since
  # mmsd-tng left with chatty; the repo stays, because megapixels still needs
  # it.
  #
  # Packages Omarchy installs that are x86_64-only are listed at the bottom as
  # comments, with the reason.

  # --- The device -------------------------------------------------------------
  # A virtual name, never a real one (docs/devices.md D5). Exactly one
  # moarchy-device-* package provides it, and that package is the only thing in
  # the project that names a kernel, a bootloader or a modem daemon.
  #
  # Depending on the virtual name is what keeps THIS list device-independent.
  # It also turns "built a rootfs and forgot the device" into an unsatisfied
  # dependency at pacstrap time -- a build failure with a name on it, rather
  # than an image that boots to a shell at the wrong scale with no modem.
  'moarchy-device'

  # --- Compositor and session -------------------------------------------------
  'sway'
  'swaybg'
  'swayidle'
  'swaylock'
  'wl-clipboard'

  # --- The shell -------------------------------------------------------------
  # In 4.x the bar, launcher, notifications and OSD are ONE quickshell/QML shell
  # (shipped as shell/ inside the Omarchy repo). waybar, mako, walker, elephant
  # and swayosd are all gone -- do not reintroduce them, they would fight it.
  'quickshell'
  'inotify-tools'
  'xdg-desktop-portal-wlr'
  'xdg-desktop-portal-gtk'
  'polkit-gnome'
  # The secret service. It had to sit ABOVE geary in this list, and geary has
  # gone (see Email, below), but the rule outlives it: anything that depends
  # on the virtual org.freedesktop.secrets has to come after this line.
  # pacman resolves this array top to bottom, and when geary came first
  # nothing in the transaction provided the name yet, so pacman picked the
  # alphabetically first provider in the sync dbs -- chipass, a Qt password
  # manager with a drawer tile of its own -- and installed it beside
  # gnome-keyring. Measured 2026-09-16 in the builder container, resolving this
  # array with geary before gnome-keyring and after: chipass in the plan, then
  # not. image/verify.sh checks for it.
  'gnome-keyring'
  'wlsunset'

  # --- GTK theming ------------------------------------------------------------
  # libadwaita's stylesheet, ported to GTK3. The GTK4 apps take the theme from
  # default/themed/gtk.css.tpl on their own, because libadwaita reads the user
  # stylesheet's named colours; GTK3's built-in Adwaita bakes its colours in at
  # build time and ignores them (basecamp/omarchy#7557 measured it), so Geary
  # and the portal's file chooser need a theme whose rules do read the names.
  # bin/omarchy-theme-set-gnome points GTK3 at it, and only when it is here:
  # naming a theme that is not installed drops every GTK3 app to the fallback
  # rather than leaving it on Adwaita. `any` arch, so ALARM has it.
  'adw-gtk-theme'

  # --- Terminal ---------------------------------------------------------------
  # One, and it is the one everything already used. bin/moarchy-launch-tui execs
  # `foot` by name and pkgbuilds/moarchy declares it, so every TUI, every agent
  # window, the config editor and the removal prompt were foot before this list
  # said so. Themed by upstream's foot.ini.tpl, and it turns a tap into a
  # left-button click (`man 1 foot`, TOUCHSCREEN), which is what the shade's TUI
  # cards rely on and what makes it usable as THE terminal rather than only as
  # the one scripts open.
  #
  # alacritty and qmlkonsole were both here until 2026-09-08 and neither was a
  # second choice so much as a second tile (docs/apps.md, "One terminal"):
  #
  #   alacritty   7.75 MiB, eight times the other two together, to be an OpenGL
  #               terminal on a GLES 2.0 Mali-400. `pacman -Qi` said Required By
  #               moarchy-meta and Optional For None, and the only path to it was
  #               bin/moarchy-launch-terminal's fallback chain -- behind
  #               xdg-terminal-exec, which hung. It kept "Omarchy fidelity",
  #               which is not a thing a phone screen can show.
  #   qmlkonsole  935 KiB and touch-friendly, which foot also is. It had won the
  #               xdg default by being the only TerminalEmulator that claimed it,
  #               and that is the whole reason omarchy-launch-about answers
  #               `Unknown option 'render'` and bin/moarchy-about exists.
  #
  # Both still install: `pacman -S alacritty qmlkonsole`. Their Settings rows are
  # guarded on presence and come back with them.
  'foot'

  # --- Capture ----------------------------------------------------------------
  'grim'
  'slurp'
  'satty'

  # --- Audio ------------------------------------------------------------------
  'pipewire'
  'pipewire-pulse'
  'wireplumber'
  'pamixer'
  'playerctl'
  # The browser's media decoding, and it is here rather than in optdepends
  # BECAUSE amberol left the set. webkitgtk-6.0 and webkit2gtk-4.1 both list
  # gst-plugins-good as an *optional* dependency ("media decoding"), and
  # pacstrap does not install optdepends -- so while amberol was here it was the
  # only thing hard-depending on this, and Epiphany was decoding web video on a
  # library a music player happened to drag in. Remove amberol without this line
  # and `pacman -Rs` takes gst-plugins-good with it, correctly by its own rules
  # and disastrously by ours: the next image would have a browser that plays no
  # audio and no video, with nothing anywhere saying why.
  #
  # Caught on the device before it shipped -- `pacman -Qi gst-plugins-good` said
  # "Required By: amberol" and "Optional For: moarchy-meta webkit2gtk-4.1
  # webkitgtk-6.0", which is the whole bug in three lines. It stays off
  # gst-plugins-ugly and gst-libav, which are the patent-encumbered and
  # nonfree halves and are still optdepends below.
  'gst-plugins-good'
  # No wiremix. It was the audio mixer until moarchy.settings grew two screens
  # of its own (Pages.js "sound.output"/"sound.input", and bin/moarchy-audio
  # behind them), and both files already say in their headers that they replaced
  # it -- a TUI whose window mapped at 47 columns on this panel. It stayed in
  # the set after the thing that replaced it shipped, which is the only reason
  # it was still here.

  # --- Touch input ------------------------------------------------------------
  # Sway delivers wl_touch to clients and stops there: it has no on-screen
  # keyboard, and `bindgesture` only ever sees libinput gesture events, which
  # touchpads produce and touchscreens do not.
  #
  # moarchy-keyboard is the keyboard. It binds zwp_input_method_v2 for apps that
  # speak text-input-v3 and zwp_virtual_keyboard_v1 for everything that does not,
  # keeps ONE layer surface for the life of the process, and reads the Omarchy
  # palette straight out of ~/.local/state/omarchy/current/theme/colors.toml, so
  # it recolours live on omarchy-theme-set like the bar and the drawer.
  #
  # Built by docker/build-packages.sh from the pin in manifest.toml and
  # published to the [moarchy] repo. It was deliberately absent from this list
  # while there was no repo for pacman to find it in; there is one now, so it
  # is an ordinary dependency like everything else.
  'moarchy-keyboard'
  #
  # squeekboard was here and behaved correctly -- self-raising, one surface, a
  # terminal layout -- but its styling is GTK CSS baked into its gresource, so the
  # largest thing on screen while typing stayed Adwaita grey through every theme
  # change, and GTK3 draws through cairo, which on this GPU means the CPU. wvkbd
  # was here before that and leaked a layer surface per activation.
  #
  # libbsd STAYS, despite arriving only because squeekboard's Arch Linux ARM
  # package fails to declare it. Removing it with squeekboard fails: sndio depends
  # on it, so pacman refuses. Left listed rather than left to sndio to pull in, so
  # that the dependency is visible rather than incidental.
  'libbsd'

  # layer-shell-qt is the one genuinely new runtime dependency: the keyboard is a
  # Qt client and needs it to make its panel a layer surface.
  'layer-shell-qt'

  # lisgd is gone with bin/moarchy-gestures (docs/refactor.md A1, A2). It was
  # kept for one release after moarchy.gestures replaced it, on the reading
  # that the old edge-swipe bindings were worth a way back to; nothing started
  # the script, so what the dependency actually bought was a [danctnix] package
  # in the image to support a file git history already holds.

  # --- Hardware ---------------------------------------------------------------
  'brightnessctl'
  'networkmanager'
  'bluez'
  'bluez-utils'

  # --- CLI / TUI (the Omarchy idiom, and what is actually usable on this SoC) --
  'bat'
  'bluetui'
  'btop'
  # btop will not draw below 60 columns and a tiled terminal here is 47. bottom
  # (btm) reflows, so it is the one that actually works beside the bar, and
  # s-tui is the other docs/apps.md name as installed.
  #
  # No htop. Three process viewers for one phone was two too many, and htop was
  # the one with nothing of its own: btop is the one people ask for by name,
  # btm is the one that fits the width, and moarchy.device draws the same four
  # numbers with no terminal at all.
  'bottom'
  's-tui'
  'dust'
  'expac'
  'eza'
  'fastfetch'
  'fd'
  'fzf'
  'git'
  'gum'
  'impala'
  'jq'
  'lazygit'
  'less'
  'man-db'
  # No neovim, and this one is not just a name leaving a list -- it was the
  # default editor, so removing it is a change of behaviour that took four files
  # with it. The Text Editor plugin (moarchy.editor, in moarchy
  # further down) is the editor now: it is the GUI answer to "the system wants
  # me to edit something", which on a phone with no keyboard attached is the
  # only answer that is any use. It was gnome-text-editor until 2026-09-16.
  #
  # What had to move with it, because upstream's two scripts both hardcode nvim
  # as the fallback and neither can reach a GUI editor safely:
  #   bin/omarchy-default-editor   accepts the name at all, and answers it when
  #                                nothing has been chosen
  #   bin/omarchy-launch-editor    falls back to something installed, and runs a
  #                                GUI editor so that it BLOCKS when it is
  #                                $EDITOR -- without which `git commit` reads
  #                                an unedited template. For the plugin that is
  #                                moarchy-editor --wait, since a plugin is not
  #                                a process to wait on
  # Both shadow upstream by PATH order, like the other 22 in bin/.
  'plocate'
  'ripgrep'
  'starship'
  'tldr'
  'tmux'
  'unzip'
  'zoxide'

  # --- Coding agents ----------------------------------------------------------
  # None of these is an agent. They are what turns "install Grok" from a
  # research project into a row in Settings.
  #
  # Everything else is already here: omarchy-config ships upstream's whole bin/
  # to /usr/bin, so omarchy-agent, omarchy-default-agent and omarchy-mise-install
  # are on PATH, and moarchy.settings already draws Apps > AI agent with a row
  # per agent. All of it is built on mise, and mise is the one name Arch Linux
  # ARM has not got -- aarch64's extra.db carries nodejs and npm and no mise at
  # all. Built from the pin in manifest.toml like yay and the rest of the AUR
  # half.
  'mise-bin'
  #
  # Node, from the repos rather than from mise. Upstream never installs it with
  # pacman -- install/user/mise-work.sh runs `mise use -g node@latest`, and its
  # offline path unpacks a bundled node-v*-linux-x64.tar.gz, which is x86_64 by
  # filename. Taking the ALARM build instead costs ~50 MB in the image and means
  # the first run of an agent downloads the agent, not a language runtime as
  # well, over whatever connection a phone happens to have.
  #
  # npm is a separate package here, and it is needed rather than incidental:
  # mise's npm: backend shells out to it, and npm:@xai-official/grok is exactly
  # what omarchy-default-agent asks mise for.
  'nodejs'
  'npm'

  # --- Telephony -------------------------------------------------------------
  # Calls and SMS. The modem's own power and audio profile are the device
  # package's (moarchy-device-*); these are the layer above. The apps are the
  # moarchy.phone and moarchy.messages plugins in moarchy,
  # further down, and they reach the modem through processes rather than a
  # library, because Quickshell has no D-Bus module:
  #   modemmanager  mmcli, and the system bus signals a gdbus monitor waits on
  #   callaudiod    earpiece/speaker/mute during a call, over busctl --user
  # fbcli (feedbackd, which pkgbuilds/moarchy declares), gdbus (glib2) and
  # setpriv (util-linux) are the rest, and none of them needs a line here.
  #
  # gnome-calls, chatty and mmsd-tng were here until 2026-09-16. Calls and
  # Chatty were a process and a daemon apiece, ~115 MB resident together, and
  # the daemons needed same-named replacement units in pkgbuilds/moarchy
  # because both shipped `Requisite=gnome-session-initialized.target`, which
  # under sway never exists. The plugins are the daemon now: the shell keeps
  # them loaded. They must also not come back beside the plugins rather than
  # instead of them -- Chatty takes every text off the modem and deletes it, so
  # with it running Messages never sees one. mmsd-tng was MMS transport for
  # Chatty; Messages is SMS only, so nothing on the image would talk to it.
  'modemmanager'
  'callaudiod'

  # --- Apps -------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The set docs/apps.md documents as working, and the reason it is in the *base*
  # list rather than the extras one: without it a fresh install has eight
  # launchable entries, six of which are terminals, and pulling the drawer up
  # gives you an app grid with nothing in it. A phone with no clock, no file
  # manager and no browser is not finished -- and those three are now the
  # Quickshell plugins in moarchy, not a GTK/KDE process apiece.
  #
  # These were hand-installed for the docs/apps.md screenshots and never recorded
  # in any list the installer reads, so the first unattended provision wiped them
  # and the launcher came up empty.
  #
  # Both families are chosen for the 360px screen: GNOME's libadwaita and KDE's
  # Kirigami apps reflow natively, and neither needs its own session -- they are
  # ordinary Wayland clients under Sway.
  # The default calculator, calendar, clock, contacts, files, keep, text
  # editor, vitals, weather, phone, messages, mail and the rest of the
  # moarchy-apps Quickshell set. They are plugins
  # the shell already holds, so summoning one is a window becoming visible
  # rather than a process starting -- which is the whole reason they replace
  # kalk, calindori, gnome-clocks, gnome-contacts, portfolio-file-manager and
  # the GTK Keep / Vitals packages that used to sit on this line,
  # gnome-calls and chatty from the Telephony block above, and geary.
  #
  # Packaged by pkgbuilds/moarchy with the rest of the plugin tree since the
  # moarchy-apps snapshot was absorbed (docs/structure.md B6), and enabled by
  # the packaged shell.json. They arrive with 'moarchy' below; ui.catalog is a
  # kit review window and stays in moarchy-apps.

  # gnome-text-editor left 2026-09-16 for moarchy.editor, the same cut as
  # the clocks below. It installs from the store for anybody who wants tabs,
  # search and highlighting back, and omarchy-default-editor still offers it.
  #
  # loupe, papers and foliate left the same day, and not for a plugin: the
  # default set is the shell's own apps and what a phone cannot be a phone
  # without, and an image viewer, a PDF viewer and an e-book reader are each
  # a tap away in the store. Until one is installed, a picture, a PDF or an
  # .epub tapped in Files has no handler of its own, and goes wherever
  # xdg-open's fallbacks send it -- not yet looked at on a device.
  # gnome-clocks, portfolio-file-manager, kalk, calindori and kweather left
  # 2026-09-15: the moarchy.{clock,files,calculator,calendar,weather}
  # plugins cover the same ground and are already loaded. kclock and index-fm
  # had already gone for being a second clock and a second file manager; this
  # is the same cut against the first of each, now that there is a first that
  # is ours. upower stays: moarchy declares it, so removing the weather app
  # cannot take the battery indicator (the failure that put upower in
  # pkgbuilds/moarchy in the first place).
  'keysmith'
  # qmlkonsole removed 2026-09-08 with alacritty, for the reason the Terminal
  # section above gives: foot is the terminal, and this was a second tile for it.

  # WebKit, ~260 MB, and the URL bar is at the bottom where a thumb is. Firefox
  # and Chromium both run and both cost far more memory for no layout benefit.
  'epiphany'

  # Maps. Adaptive GTK4/libadwaita, so it reflows to 360px like the rest of the
  # GNOME set.
  'gnome-maps'

  # Added 2026-09-06 to close five gaps the set above left open. They are in
  # docs/apps.md's tables carrying a dagger, which is that file's way of saying
  # "in the set, not launched on the device yet": an unmarked row there means
  # installed, run and screenshotted uncropped, and none of these have been.
  # They are here on availability and fit, and the dagger comes off when
  # somebody has seen one run.
  #
  # None of the five needs building. Arch Linux ARM has all of extra for
  # aarch64, so the whole GNOME adaptive set and the whole Plasma Mobile set
  # are already binaries -- the AUR is not the constraint for app choice, the
  # 360px screen is. Every dependency of all five resolves inside the repos
  # the image already enables; nothing new is turned on for them.

  # gnome-contacts left with the others above. The plugin writes
  # ~/.local/share/moarchy-contacts/contacts.json, not evolution-data-server,
  # and until 2026-09-16 that left Calls and Chats showing bare numbers,
  # because evolution-data-server was the only store they read. Phone and
  # Messages read Contacts' file, so the gap closed with them rather than by
  # putting a 2.3 MB GTK app back on the image.

  # Email is moarchy.mail, in moarchy, and has been since
  # 2026-09-16. geary was here until then. moarchy-store's sweep had already
  # rejected it for this screen -- "desktop-shaped three-pane mail client.
  # Also wants an unlocked keyring" -- and where no keyring was unlocked, its
  # prompt mapped behind its own window. It still installs with pacman for
  # anybody who wants it; the store does not list it.
  #
  # (The obvious-looking pick from [danctnix] was geary-mobile, and it is a
  # trap: "This is a dummy package. It is no longer needed." The same is true
  # of gnome-contacts-mobile and gnome-calendar-mobile.)
  #
  # Mail keeps its password in a 0600 file rather than the keyring, for the
  # keyring's reason above, and talks to the server through moarchy-mail, which
  # is Python's standard library and nothing else. python is moarchy's
  # dependency, not a line here.

  # No RSS reader. alligator was here -- Kirigami, 0.9 MB, chosen over newsflash
  # for its size -- and it is out with the rest of the media set below: a
  # default image should carry what a phone cannot be a phone without, and a
  # feed reader is a preference. It installs from the store in one tap.

  # No music player. amberol was here and the reasoning that put it here still
  # holds if one is ever wanted back -- it beats gnome-music because it drags in
  # no localsearch/tinysparql indexer daemon to crawl storage on a 1.15 GHz A53,
  # and it beats decibels because decibels plays one file with no queue. It is
  # out because it is a preference rather than a necessity, and it is one tap
  # away in the store.

  # No password manager. secrets was here until 2026-09-16 -- KeePass v4
  # .kdbx, which keysmith is not, since keysmith holds TOTP codes only -- and
  # left with loupe, papers and foliate: a preference, not something a phone is
  # unfinished without, and one tap away in the store. keepassxc is still the
  # wrong alternative for the reason that put secrets here: a Qt Widgets
  # desktop app that does not reflow to 360px.

  # No songrec -- and as of 2026-09-19 the reason has expired, which is worth
  # saying out loud rather than leaving a stale exclusion to look deliberate.
  # It went because "listen to what is playing" needs a microphone and the
  # PinePhone's recorded digital silence (RMS 0 at PipeWire and raw ALSA
  # alike). That device is gone, and sargo's microphone demonstrably works --
  # D32 is a call with voice on it in both directions.
  #
  # Deliberately NOT put back in the same change that removed the device: this
  # release is a deletion and adding a package to the default set is a decision
  # about what a phone ships with, not a consequence of dropping another phone.
  # It is one name in `depends` when somebody decides. It is in the store
  # meanwhile.

  # --- Camera -----------------------------------------------------------
  # Megapixels, rebuilt here (pkgbuilds/megapixels) rather than taken from
  # [danctnix]: the packaged build looks a profile up by a name this device
  # does not have, which is D30's green preview. It is the only camera app that
  # works here, and the reason is libmegapixels -- it reads a device config
  # describing the media graph and configures the links itself before
  # streaming. A generic app never does, which is why `snapshot` and
  # `plasma-camera` cannot work at all, and why the old "VIDIOC_STREAMON fails"
  # note was really "nothing was configuring the pipeline". libdng and
  # libmegapixels still come from [danctnix]; Arch Linux ARM has neither.
  # See docs/apps.md.
  'megapixels'

  # Required BY megapixels and declared by nothing. Without it ~/Pictures never
  # exists and every photo is captured and then silently discarded at the last
  # step -- the burst goes to /tmp first, so the failure appears only after the
  # shutter animation and the app reports nothing at all.
  'xdg-user-dirs'

  # The Linux Command Library GUI -- a Qt6 command reference, useful on a device
  # whose terminal is 47 columns wide. Built from the pin in manifest.toml; it
  # is not in ALARM.
  'lcl-gui-bin'

  # --- Fonts ------------------------------------------------------------------
  'noto-fonts'
  'noto-fonts-emoji'
  'ttf-jetbrains-mono-nerd'

  # --- Support ----------------------------------------------------------------
  # gnome-keyring is not here; it is under The shell, and the position matters.
  'libsecret'
  'imagemagick'
  'mesa-demos'
  'mesa-utils'
  'openssh'
  'sudo'

  # ============================================================================
  # Deliberately NOT installed
  # ============================================================================
  # hyprland, hyprlock, hypridle, hyprsunset, hyprpicker
  #   -> These DO build for aarch64, but Hyprland hard-requires a GLES 3.0 context
  #      (src/render/OpenGL.cpp: RASSERT on "either GLES3.2 or 3.0"). The Mali-400
  #      MP2 in the Allwinner A64 is GLES 2.0 only. Replaced by sway/swaylock/
  #      swayidle/wlsunset/grim+slurp.
  #
  # 1password-beta, 1password-cli, spotify, obsidian, typora, dotnet-runtime-9.0,
  # aether, cliamp, asdcontrol, tzupdate, ufw-docker
  #   -> x86_64-only (Omarchy's own repo has no aarch64 tree:
  #      pkgs.omarchy.org/stable/aarch64/omarchy.db returns 404), and none are
  #      sensible on a 720x1440 phone screen.
  #
  # tesseract / tesseract-data-eng
  #   -> Not available for aarch64. OCR capture is dropped.

  # waydroid
  #   -> Runs Android in an LXC container, and it is the only route to the apps
  #      that have no Linux client at all. Removed 2026-09-06, the same day it
  #      was added, and NOT for the usual reason: the kernel prerequisite is
  #      actually met. linux-megi 6.18.39 enables ANDROID_BINDER_IPC,
  #      ANDROID_BINDERFS and ANDROID_BINDER_DEVICES and ships zero binder .ko
  #      among its 1536 modules, so binder is built in -- nothing to modprobe,
  #      no binder_linux-dkms, which is the usual reason Waydroid will not
  #      start on Arch. Verified from danctnix.files, so nobody has to redo it.
  #
  #      It is out because of what it costs, not what it lacks: `waydroid init`
  #      pulls a ~1 GB LineageOS arm64 image on first run, it wants a
  #      waydroid-container.service that must not be enabled until after that,
  #      and the container shares 2 GB of RAM and a GLES 2.0 Mali-400. A
  #      dependency should not commit a fresh phone to any of that.
  #
  #      `sudo pacman -S waydroid` installs it whenever someone wants to try.
  #
  # walker, elephant
  #   -> Not used in 4.x at all; the quickshell shell replaces them.
  #
  # yay, xdg-terminal-exec, ttf-ia-writer
  #   -> Not in ALARM. Built from source; see docker/build-packages.sh and the
  #      [aur.*] pins in manifest.toml. They are dependencies now, not a
  #      separate step -- the paragraph below is the current version of this
  #      one, which is kept because the reasoning above it still holds.

  # yay, xdg-terminal-exec, ttf-ia-writer and cbonsai were in that same
  # position: no aarch64 build anywhere, so the old installer built them on the
  # phone or copied tarballs in over scp. They are built once from the pins in
  # manifest.toml and published to the [moarchy] repo, so here they are
  # dependencies rather than a separate install step.
  'yay'
  'xdg-terminal-exec'
  'ttf-ia-writer'
  'cbonsai'

  # What yay needs to build anything. Without these it gets all the way to the
  # build and stops at `==> ERROR: Cannot find the fakeroot binary.` -- Settings
  # > Packages > Install from AUR lists all 119,015 packages, lets you pick one,
  # and then fails in a way that reads as a dead package rather than a missing
  # toolchain. `base` already carries gcc, binutils, libtool, gettext, file,
  # findutils, gawk, grep, gzip, sed and which; these ten are the rest of what
  # makepkg calls. 79.9 MiB installed, 13.0 MiB of it downloaded.
  #
  # Named one by one rather than as 'base-devel', which is a package rather than
  # a group now and would be the obvious single line. It depends on debugedit,
  # which depends on gdb, which pulls source-highlight and boost-libs: 105 MiB
  # of debugger on top of the 80 MiB that does the work. None of it would ever
  # run -- /etc/makepkg.conf ships !debug, and makepkg's
  # executable/debugedit.sh only looks for the binary under that option, so
  # debugedit is reachable here only by someone who edits OPTIONS, and
  # `pacman -S debugedit` is their answer.
  #
  # What naming them costs: `pacman -Qi base-devel` says not installed, and an
  # AUR PKGBUILD that lists base-devel in makedepends -- against Arch's own
  # guidelines, but it happens -- makes yay pull the whole thing at that point.
  #
  # guile is not in this list and is 54.7 MiB of the 79.9: Arch's make depends
  # on it, so it arrives whether or not anyone wants a Scheme interpreter on a
  # 2 GB phone. gc comes with it.
  'm4'
  'autoconf'
  'automake'
  'bison'
  'fakeroot'
  'flex'
  'make'
  'patch'
  'pkgconf'
  'texinfo'

  # The key that signs this repository. Without it pacman cannot validate a
  # single package, so SigLevel = Required would refuse everything.
  'moarchy-keyring'

  # The phone layer itself, and the store. moarchy pulls omarchy-config in
  # turn, so this one name brings the whole environment.
  'moarchy'
  # Named -git because that is what its PKGBUILD builds. It is the one entry
  # here that manifest.toml cannot fully pin: a VCS package fetches at HEAD
  # when makepkg runs. See [moarchy-store] there.
  'moarchy-store-git'
)

optdepends=(
  # Megapixels' video path: movie.sh -> mpegize.py, GStreamer x264enc into
  # ~/Videos. Not depends, because video is unverified on this device and these
  # are ~100 MB for a feature that records silence anyway while the microphone
  # reads RMS 0. Stills need none of them.
  'python-gobject'
  # gst-plugins-good moved up into depends when amberol left -- see there. Only
  # the ugly half is optional now.
  'gst-plugins-ugly'

  # Not installed by default. All verified available for aarch64, but each is
  # heavy for an Allwinner A64 with 2-3 GB of RAM.
  #
  #   pacman -S --needed <name>

  'chromium'
  'nautilus'
  'mpv'
  'imv'
  'signal-desktop'
  'kdenlive'
  'libreoffice-fresh'
  'gpu-screen-recorder'

  # Needed only by bin/moarchy-touch, which synthesises touchscreen input
  # through /dev/uinput so bin/moarchy-selftest --gestures can drive the
  # gesture drawer without a finger. Not needed to run the phone, only to test it.
  'python-evdev'
)

package() {
  # Intentionally empty. The dependency list is the whole package.
  :
}
